  "response": "Interior camera footage showing her gaze directed downward rather than at the roadway supported it. The physical record was assembled from police measurements, roadway video, and the car's own sensor captures. Either Rafaela Vasquez was convicted of a criminal offense arising from the crash, or Rafaela Vasquez was never convicted of any criminal offense arising from the crash; the two cannot both be true, and one of them must hold. Each change of category reset the system's prediction of her path, so the system never projected her movement across its lane. The NTSB determined that the probable cause of the crash was the safety driver's failure to monitor the driving environment due to distraction by her personal cell phone. An Uber self-driving test vehicle struck and killed Elaine Herzberg in Tempe, Arizona, on March 18, 2018.\n\nBy contrast, the automated system applied full emergency braking two seconds before impact. The automated driving system first detected Herzberg about 5.6 seconds before impact. It did so by treating the automated system's misclassification as the hazard the operator was obligated to intercept, not as an independent sufficient cause. The evidence on the human side proved decisive in a different way. Its central judgment, however, rested on the premise that a safety driver exists to catch exactly the kind of perceptual error the logs revealed. With those conditions fixed, analysts turned to the data the test vehicle had logged. Uber had disabled the Volvo's factory-installed automatic emergency braking during autonomous operation.\n\nInvestigators had to decide whether responsibility lay chiefly with the software or with the human operator assigned to supervise it. An attentive operator would have had time to brake. Investigators did not, however, treat the software record as self-contained, because a supervising human was present precisely to compensate for such errors. Herzberg was pushing a bicycle across Mill Avenue at night when she was struck, whereas the collision occurred during daylight hours in downtown Phoenix. The corporate response came later than the NTSB's probable-cause finding: after that finding, Uber suspended its self-driving vehicle testing on public roads after the crash. The National Transportation Safety Board weighed these strands together and settled the tension between the machine's failure and the operator's failure. Because the system detected her that early, it alternately classified Herzberg as a vehicle, a bicycle, and an unknown object over the remaining seconds. That streaming record confirms the driver's inattention. The Board catalogued the classification deficiencies and Uber's weak safety culture as contributing factors.\n\nAnswering that question required two reconstructions: the physical circumstances of the crossing, and the vehicle's internal record of perception and decision. In other words, the Volvo's built-in automatic emergency braking was switched off whenever Uber's self-driving system controlled the car. The analysis therefore had to account for at least one of two failures, perhaps both: the misclassification in the perception system, or a lapse by the person behind the wheel, who was not watching the road in the seconds before the collision. The investigative method, in sum, attributed the fatality chiefly to human inattention. The perception logs formed the core of the technical inquiry. The braking architecture therefore determined how much time remained for intervention, and with it how heavily the outcome depended on the operator. Even though the software's classification failure was firmly established, Tempe police reported that the safety driver's phone was streaming the television show The Voice before the crash. The criminal dimension of the case followed its own course in the Maricopa County courts. That oscillating categorization indicates that the automated driving system failed to correctly classify the pedestrian in time to avoid the collision. That determination did not excuse the software. The vehicle's configuration added a further dimension to the inquiry.",
